# Copy this file to .env.local and fill in real values. NEVER commit .env.local.
#
# Both are optional. Leave them unset and the widget still works fully for quoting, building, and
# signing — delivery on the destination chain just won't be automatic, exactly as running the
# widget with no relayer-url at all (see WidgetEmbed's own graceful-degradation copy). That is the
# realistic state until a real relayer instance exists for this environment: there is no
# publicly-deployed Ferryline relayer anywhere in this project today (every relayer URL in the docs
# is a your-relayer.example.com placeholder, or a local docker-composed instance) — deploying one
# is real infrastructure work outside what this Next.js app can do on its own.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is a Next.js requirement, not a style choice: only env vars with this exact
# prefix get inlined into client-side code, and the widget (a browser custom element) is what
# actually reads these. That also means both values are visible in this page's own Network tab to
# any visitor, by design — see below for why that changes what's safe to put in
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FERRYLINE_RELAYER_API_KEY specifically.

# The relayer's own base URL (e.g. https://your-relayer.example.com).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FERRYLINE_RELAYER_URL=

# IMPORTANT before a real launch: this MUST be a dedicated, low-privilege, easily-revocable API key
# for THIS playground specifically — never a production integrator's key. Because this value ships
# to every visitor's browser (see above), it should be treated as public the moment this page is
# deployed: provision one key just for this deployment (packages/relayer's ApiKeyStore/api_keys
# table supports per-key revocation for exactly this reason), so if it's scraped and abused,
# revoking it doesn't affect any real integrator. The relayer's own registration-time rate limit
# (FERRYLINE_REGISTRATION_LIMIT_MAX_ATTEMPTS, default 60/60s) then becomes this whole playground's
# combined budget, not a limit per visitor.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FERRYLINE_RELAYER_API_KEY=
